# AVD-AWS-0164 — map_public_ip_on_launch on public subnet
# Lab tradeoff: no NAT gateway to keep monthly cost ~$36 (see ADR-002).
# Nodes need outbound internet access for apt, kubeadm image pulls, and
# AWS API calls. Without a NAT gateway the only viable path is a public
# subnet with auto-assigned public IPs. Production setup would use private
# subnets + NAT gateway, or VPC endpoints for AWS service traffic.
AVD-AWS-0164

# AVD-AWS-0104 — egress 0.0.0.0/0 on cluster security groups
# Lab tradeoff: kubeadm nodes require unrestricted outbound access for
# container image pulls (registry.k8s.io, quay.io, docker.io), AWS API
# calls (EC2, SSM, STS), and dynamic addon manifests fetched at bootstrap.
# Restricting egress on a generic kubeadm cluster requires enumerating
# every registry and AWS endpoint upfront, which is brittle and out of
# scope for a lab. Production setup would use VPC endpoints (ECR, S3,
# EC2, STS, SSM) and allow-listed egress CIDRs for image registries.
AVD-AWS-0104

# AVD-AWS-0107 — SSH/RDP ingress from 0.0.0.0/0 on cluster security groups
# Lab tradeoff: my_ip defaults to 0.0.0.0/0 because the operator's home IP
# changes constantly. For a short-lived lab (destroyed nightly by cron) SSH
# keys provide the real access control. Production setup would restrict to a
# known CIDR or use AWS SSM Session Manager instead of direct SSH.
AVD-AWS-0107

# AVD-AWS-0132 — S3 bucket not encrypted with a customer managed key
# Ratified in brief #S2-1: the backups bucket uses SSE-S3, matching the
# tfstate bucket (bootstrap-aws.sh). A CMK adds KMS cost and key-policy
# complexity with no threat-model gain here: the key would live in the SAME
# lab account as the data, so it does not change the blast radius of an
# account compromise. Revisit if backups ever hold data with compliance
# requirements (real go-live, not a lab).
AVD-AWS-0132
